{"id":"waiver-departments","title":"Departments that skip competition most, relative to what they buy","question":"For each department, what share of its recorded purchasing went through a route that sets competition aside?","finding":"Citywide the share is 7.5%. At Homelessness Services it is 43% ($1.45 billion of $3.34 billion), mostly emergency powers; Assessor - Recorder follows at 24% and Health Service System at 17%. 1 of the 39 departments compared put a third or more of their recorded purchasing through such routes.","how":"Payments from fiscal 2019 to fiscal 2026, by department and by the purchasing rule keyed on each payment, sorted into routes as in the first screen. The share is payments under emergency, sole-source, ordinance-waiver and other \"not bid\" rules, divided by all of the department’s payments that record a rule. Departments with less than $25 million of such payments are left out.","limits":"Each of these routes is written into the Administrative Code with a stated condition: a real emergency, a real single source, a class of work the Board has excused from bidding. A department whose work is homelessness or behavioural health was given its waiver by the Board and is expected to rank high; the question it raises is for the Board. The last column shows how much of each department’s spending records no rule at all, which limits the comparison. The payment file labels each line with a fiscal year that is not always the year the payment was issued: some earlier payments, mostly on purchase orders in multi-year capital funds, are filed under a later year, and the Controller's own published figures follow that label. Payments are dated here by voucher number, a single running sequence, which moves $5.52 billion of $119.3 billion (4.6%) to an earlier year than its label; $1.08 billion of that belongs to fiscal 2018 or earlier, before the file starts. Near the turn of a year the number dates a payment to within a few days. Payments that belong to fi